    <h1>Smoked Paprika vs Chilli Powder: Heat, Colour &amp; When to Use Which</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      They share a deep red colour, but they do opposite jobs. Smoked paprika is a low-heat sweet
      pepper for smoke and colour; chilli powder is a ground hot pepper for heat. Know which you
      actually need before you specify.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Smoked paprika and chilli powder share a deep red colour but do opposite jobs: smoked paprika is a low-heat sweet pepper delivering smoke and colour, while chilli powder is a ground hot pepper delivering heat. Confusing them is the most common specification error in spice buying.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The two-minute answer</h2>
    <p>
      Smoked paprika is a <strong>sweet pepper</strong> (under 1,000 SHU) that has been smoke-dried,
      giving it a smoky, sweet, deep-red profile. Chilli powder is a <strong>ground hot pepper</strong>
      whose heat — 10,000 to 50,000+ SHU — is the whole point. One is a colour-and-smoke ingredient;
      the other is a heat ingredient. They are not interchangeable.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What smoked paprika actually is</h2>
    <p>
      Smoked paprika (in Spain, <em>pimentón</em>) is made from mild red peppers that are dried over
      smoke — traditionally oak — before grinding. The result is a sweet, earthy powder with a
      distinctive smoky note and strong red colour. Its heat is negligible: sweet smoked paprika sits
      under 500 SHU, and even the "picante" hot version stays around 500–1,000 SHU. Its value is
      <strong>smoke flavour and red colour (ASTA)</strong>, not heat.
    </p>
    <p>
      For sourcing, note that authentic oak-smoked <em>Pimentón de la Vera</em> is a protected Spanish
      origin product. Most industrial volume is a "smoke-dried style" made outside Spain — a
      cost-effective alternative, but not the same PDO product. If colour and sweetness are your
      targets, the sweet-pepper specialists at
      <a href="https://paprikabulk.com/china-paprika-powder/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">paprikabulk.com</a>
      cover that range.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What chilli powder actually is</h2>
    <p>
      Chilli powder is ground dried <em>hot</em> pepper. Its defining property is heat, measured in
      Scoville Heat Units (SHU): medium blends run 10,000–20,000 SHU, high heat 20,000–50,000 SHU,
      and extra-hot varieties exceed 50,000 SHU. Heat is non-linear and variety-dependent, which is
      why a real specification names the variety and its SHU window — see our
      <a href="/heat-levels/high-heat/">high heat</a> and
      <a href="/heat-levels/extra-hot/">extra hot</a> reference pages.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Heat is the deciding difference</h2>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #d8c9c0;text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px;">Property</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Smoked paprika</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Chilli powder</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Base pepper</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Mild sweet pepper</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Hot chilli (variety-named)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Heat (SHU)</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Under 1,000</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">10,000 – 50,000+</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Job</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Smoke + red colour</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Heat + chilli flavour</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Typical use</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">BBQ rubs, plant-based meats, dips</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Hot sauces, seasonings, snacks</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">When to use which</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You want heat</strong> → chilli powder, specified by variety and SHU window. Smoked paprika cannot deliver meaningful heat.</li>
      <li><strong>You want smoke and a deep red colour</strong> → smoked paprika. Chilli powder carries no smoke note.</li>
      <li><strong>You want both</strong> → use them together: smoked paprika for colour and smoke, a small dose of a high-SHU chilli for heat.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How the production differs</h2>
    <p>
      The names sound alike, but the processing is not. Smoked paprika starts as a <em>mild</em>
      pepper and is dried over smoke — the smoke does the work, not the pepper's own heat. Chilli
      powder starts as a <em>hot</em> pepper and is simply dried and ground; the heat is already in
      the fruit. One adds character through a drying method, the other through the variety itself.
      Understanding that distinction is what prevents the wrong purchase.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">A note on the name "chilli powder"</h2>
    <p>
      Be aware that "chilli powder" is ambiguous across regions. In many European and Asian markets
      it means <strong>pure ground chilli</strong> (heat is the point). In the US, "chili powder" is
      often a <em>blend</em> — ground chilli mixed with cumin, oregano, garlic and salt for the dish
      "chili". When you write a specification, state whether you mean pure ground chilli or a seasoned
      blend; the two are different products with different uses and different SHU behaviour.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the difference between smoked paprika and chilli powder?</h3>
      <p>Smoked paprika is a low-heat sweet pepper (under 1,000 SHU) that is smoke-dried for a smoky, sweet, deep-red profile and used mainly for colour and barbecue flavour. Chilli powder is a ground hot pepper whose heat — 10,000 to 50,000+ SHU — is the point. One is a colour and smoke ingredient; the other is a heat ingredient.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Is smoked paprika hot?</h3>
      <p>No, not in chilli terms. Sweet smoked paprika is under 500 SHU and even the 'picante' hot version sits around 500–1,000 SHU — mild sweet-pepper territory, far below chilli powder. Its character is smoke and colour, not heat.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can I substitute chilli powder for smoked paprika?</h3>
      <p>Not one-for-one. They sit at opposite ends of the heat scale. A 20,000–50,000 SHU chilli powder added at smoked-paprika quantities will overwhelm a dish with heat, and it carries no smoke note. Use chilli powder for heat and smoked paprika for smoke and colour.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which one should I use for heat?</h3>
      <p>Chilli powder. Smoked paprika tops out around 1,000 SHU — it cannot deliver meaningful heat. If heat is the goal, specify the chilli variety and its SHU window, not a generic 'chilli powder'.</p>
    </div>

